    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.local_efficiency - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.local_efficiency.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.local_efficiency()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Calculate the local efficiency of the graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.local_efficiency()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Calculate the local efficiency of the graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.local_efficiency()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Calculate the local efficiency of the graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCalculate the local efficiency of the graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L94932-L95052){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.local_efficiency()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Local efficiency](../assets/graphs/local-efficiency.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"The shared seven-node graph shaded by each node's local efficiency, how well its neighbors stay connected without it: A 1.00, B and D 0.83, C 0.72, E 0.33, F and G 0. The method returns the mean over all nodes, 0.53, highlighted at the bottom of the table.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nLocal efficiency is the average global efficiency of each node's neighborhood subgraph. It\nmeasures how efficiently the neighbors of a node can communicate when that node is removed.\nHigh local efficiency indicates good redundancy and fault tolerance.\n\nFor each node, the method takes the subgraph formed by its neighbors (the node itself is\nleft out), computes the average of `1 / distance` over all pairs of those neighbors using\nonly paths inside that subgraph, and then averages this value over all nodes. Neighbor\npairs that can't reach each other within the subgraph contribute `0`. Nodes with fewer\nthan two neighbors contribute `0` and are still counted in the average.\n\nLocal efficiency is used to:\n\n- quantify fault tolerance: how well a network keeps working around a failed node\n- compare the \"small-world\" character of networks together with `global_efficiency()`\n- measure local clustering in a way that also rewards indirect (2+ hop) connections\n  among neighbors\n\nDistances are hop counts; edge weights are ignored. A neighbor joined by parallel edges\ncounts once, and self-loops are ignored, so a node is never part of its own\nneighborhood. On a directed graph, a node's neighbors are its out-neighbors, paths\nbetween them follow edge direction, and each ordered pair `(u, v)` of neighbors is\ncounted separately, as in `global_efficiency()`.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The average local efficiency over all nodes (between `0` and `1`). An empty graph\n    returns `0.0`.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nFor every node $v$, the local efficiency measures how well its neighborhood $N(v)$\ncan route around it: $E_{\\mathrm{loc}}(v) = E_{\\mathrm{glob}}(G[N(v)])$, the global\nefficiency of the induced subgraph on $v$'s neighbors, and the overall statistic is\nthe average across all nodes. Nodes with $|N(v)| < 2$ contribute $0$ by convention\nsince there is nothing to route, and neighbor pairs that are disconnected within\n$G[N(v)]$ contribute $0$. High local efficiency signals a network with redundant\nlocal paths, which is closely related to the clustering coefficient but also gives\npartial credit to neighbors joined by longer paths. Total cost is\n$O(V \\cdot \\bar{k}(\\bar{k} + \\bar{e}))$ where $\\bar{k}$ and $\\bar{e}$ are average\nneighborhood sizes and edge counts.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~global_efficiency`) \n\n:   The same measure over the whole graph.\n\n[](`~clustering_coefficient`) \n\n:   Count only direct links between a node's neighbors.\n\n[](`~transitivity`) \n\n:   The share of connected triples that close into triangles.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nIn a triangle with a tail, the triangle's corners each keep a link between their\nneighbors, while the tail node has only one neighbor and adds `0`:\n\n::: {#cad98df2 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\"), (\"C\", \"D\")]\n)\n\nround(graph.local_efficiency(), 3)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n0.583\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Friends of friends\n\nFriends tend to know each other, so in a social network a person's friends are mostly\nstill in touch without them. A metro has few triangles of track, so a station's\nneighbors are rarely joined except through it. The two kinds of network reach the rest\nof the network about as well, but their local efficiency is far apart:\n\n::: {#be858913 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport polars as pl\n\nnetworks = {\n    \"friends\": ec.friends(),\n    \"karate_club\": ec.karate_club(),\n    \"les_miserables\": ec.les_miserables(),\n    \"metro\": ec.metro(),\n}\n\npl.DataFrame(\n    {\n        \"network\": list(networks),\n        \"global\": [\n            round(g.global_efficiency(), 3) for g in networks.values()\n        ],\n        \"local\": [\n            round(g.local_efficiency(), 3) for g in networks.values()\n        ],\n    }\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (4, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>network</th><th>global</th><th>local</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>f64</td><td>f64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;friends&quot;</td><td>0.422</td><td>0.529</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;karate_club&quot;</td><td>0.492</td><td>0.645</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;les_miserables&quot;</td><td>0.435</td><td>0.621</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;metro&quot;</td><td>0.404</td><td>0.058</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n`metro()` is a made-up city's metro network, each station placed at its `pos` on the map.\nIts only two triangles share the track from Central to Riverside, one with Old Town and\none with Market:\n\n::: {#ea668683 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nec.metro().plot(\n    layout=\"pos\",\n    highlight=[\n        (\"Central\", \"Riverside\"), (\"Old Town\", \"Central\"),\n        (\"Old Town\", \"Riverside\"), (\"Central\", \"Market\"),\n        (\"Market\", \"Riverside\"),\n    ],\n    label_by=\"label\",\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e